{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_019GiPJLZRv3Cf5r2bg9YXwe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Rust translation of the C library in c_src/.\n2\t//\n3\t// Public ABI (from `nm -D` on the C shared object):\n4\t//     T match\n5\t//     T spectral_contrast\n6\t//\n7\t// `preprocess`, `total`, `smoothen`, `differentiate`, `dot_product` and\n8\t// `normalize` are all `static` in the C sources and therefore local symbols\n9\t// (`t` in nm output); they are NOT part of the exported ABI.\n10\t//\n11\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n12\t// CRITICAL DETAIL: the two translation units disagree about `float_t`.\n13\t//\n14\t//   * match.c            includes \"match.h\", which does\n15\t//                            typedef double float_t;\n16\t//                        so in match.c `float_t` is `double` (8 bytes).\n17\t//\n18\t//   * spectral_contrast.c includes ONLY <math.h> and never includes match.h.\n19\t//                        On x86-64 glibc FLT_EVAL_METHOD == 0, so <math.h>\n20\t//                        supplies its own `typedef float float_t;`\n21\t//                        (verified: sizeof(float_t) == 4).\n22\t//                        So in spectral_contrast.c `float_t` is `float`.\n23\t//\n24\t// The declaration of spectral_contrast that match.c sees says `double *`, but\n25\t// the compiled definition indexes its arguments as `float *`. Because the two\n26\t// only ever agree on the ABI (a pointer is a pointer), the code compiles and\n27\t// links, but `spectral_contrast` reinterprets the bytes of the caller's\n28\t// `double` array as an array of `float`.\n29\t//\n30\t// This is a genuine bug in the original library. Per the translation rules it\n31\t// is reproduced exactly rather than fixed. Confirmed against the disassembly\n32\t// of the C shared object, which uses `movss` / `mulss` / `cvtss2sd`:\n33\t//\n34\t//     16d0: movss    (%rax),%xmm0        ; load a[i] as f32\n35\t//     16d8: mulss    %xmm0,%xmm0         ; multiply in SINGLE precision\n36\t//     16dc: cvtss2sd %xmm0,%xmm0         ; widen the f32 product to f64\n37\t//     16e0: addsd    %xmm0,%xmm4         ; accumulate in DOUBLE precision\n38\t//\n39\t// and, in `normalize`:\n40\t//\n41\t//     1763: cvtss2sd (%rcx),%xmm0        ; widen v[i] to f64\n42\t//     1767: divsd    %xmm4,%xmm0         ; divide in DOUBLE precision\n43\t//     176b: cvtsd2ss %xmm0,%xmm0         ; round the quotient back to f32\n44\t//     176f: movss    %xmm0,(%rcx)        ; store as f32\n45\t//\n46\t// Every exported function below therefore operates on raw pointers rather than\n47\t// Rust slices: the C API permits the two argument arrays to alias (e.g.\n48\t// `spectral_contrast(v, v, n)`), and building two overlapping `&mut [T]`\n49\t// would be undefined behaviour in Rust even though it is well-defined in C.\n50\t//\n51\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n52\t// VERIFICATION\n53\t//\n54\t// Differentially tested against the C shared object over 474,178 lines of\n55\t// bit-exact output (every result printed as raw IEEE-754 bits, plus hashes of\n56\t// the in-place-mutated buffers): exhaustive small sizes, 200k randomized\n57\t// realistic spectra, and an adversarial suite covering signaling NaNs,\n58\t// denormals, +/-inf, +/-0, zero-magnitude (division-by-zero) inputs,\n59\t// aliased and partially-overlapping pointers, negative lengths, and sizes up\n60\t// to 65536. All byte-identical.\n61\t//\n62\t// One caveat, inherent to the C rather than to this translation: the NaN\n63\t// *payload* that `spectral_contrast` returns is not fixed by the C source. It\n64\t// depends on the compiler's choice of SSE destination operand, which varies by\n65\t// compiler and optimization level -- eleven GCC/Clang configurations of the\n66\t// same C sources fall into three mutually disagreeing groups. This translation\n67\t// reproduces the largest group, which is also the one produced by every\n68\t// optimized GCC build (`-O1`, `-O2`, `-O3`, `-Os`, and\n69\t// `cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`) plus `clang -O1`; against those the match\n70\t// is exact, with a zero-line diff.\n71\t//\n72\t// Everything the C language actually specifies is bit-exact under all eleven\n73\t// configurations. In particular `match`'s integer return value never differed\n74\t// once across the entire corpus in any configuration, and neither did\n75\t// `spectral_contrast` on non-NaN data.\n76\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n77\t\n78\tuse std::ffi::c_int;\n79\t\n80\t/// `#define N_SMOOTH 16` from match.h -- size of the smoothing kernel.\n81\tconst N_SMOOTH: c_int = 16;\n82\t\n83\t// ===========================================================================\n84\t// Bit-exact scalar SSE arithmetic\n85\t// ===========================================================================\n86\t//\n87\t// For every finite/infinite input these helpers are plain IEEE-754 operations.\n88\t// They exist solely to pin down NaN *payload* propagation.\n89\t//\n90\t// x86 SSE two-operand arithmetic (`addsd dst, src` computing `dst = dst OP\n91\t// src`) picks its NaN result as follows: if `dst` is NaN the result is `dst`\n92\t// quieted; otherwise if `src` is NaN the result is `src` quieted. So when both\n93\t// operands are NaN the *destination* operand's payload survives.\n94\t//\n95\t// GCC compiles `sum += v[i]` with the accumulator as the destination:\n96\t//\n97\t//     addsd  (%rdi,%rax,8),%xmm2      ; xmm2(sum) = xmm2(sum) + v[i]\n98\t//\n99\t// so the FIRST NaN to enter the accumulator wins and is carried to the end.\n100\t//\n101\t// LLVM, however, is free to commute floating-point addition (it treats the NaN\n102\t// payload choice as non-deterministic) and in fact emits the reverse:\n103\t//\n104\t//     addsd  %xmm0,%xmm1              ; xmm1(product) = xmm1(product) + xmm0(sum)\n105\t//\n106\t// which makes the LAST NaN win instead. That produced a genuine byte-level\n107\t// divergence from the C library on inputs whose bytes happen to decode to NaN\n108\t// floats. Routing the arithmetic through these helpers makes the operand roles\n109\t// explicit, so the result no longer depends on LLVM's choice of operand order\n110\t// and matches GCC's on every input.\n111\t//\n112\t// Non-commutative operations (`subsd`, `divsd`) cannot be reordered by the\n113\t// compiler, but they are modelled here too so that all four agree in style.\n114\t\n115\t/// Quiet a NaN the way SSE does: force the most-significant mantissa bit on.\n116\t#[inline(always)]\n117\tfn quiet64(x: f64) -> f64 {\n118\t    f64::from_bits(x.to_bits() | 0x0008_0000_0000_0000)\n119\t}\n120\t\n121\t/// Quiet a NaN the way SSE does: force the most-significant mantissa bit on.\n122\t#[inline(always)]\n123\tfn quiet32(x: f32) -> f32 {\n124\t    f32::from_bits(x.to_bits() | 0x0040_0000)\n125\t}\n126\t\n127\t/// `addsd dst, src` -- computes `dst + src`, keeping `dst`'s NaN payload.\n128\t#[inline(always)]\n129\tfn addsd(dst: f64, src: f64) -> f64 {\n130\t    if dst.is_nan() {\n131\t        quiet64(dst)\n132\t    } else if src.is_nan() {\n133\t        quiet64(src)\n134\t    } else {\n135\t        dst + src\n136\t    }\n137\t}\n138\t\n139\t/// `subsd dst, src` -- computes `dst - src`, keeping `dst`'s NaN payload.\n140\t#[inline(always)]\n141\tfn subsd(dst: f64, src: f64) -> f64 {\n142\t    if dst.is_nan() {\n143\t        quiet64(dst)\n144\t    } else if src.is_nan() {\n145\t        quiet64(src)\n146\t    } else {\n147\t        dst - src\n148\t    }\n149\t}\n150\t\n151\t/// `mulsd dst, src` -- computes `dst * src`, keeping `dst`'s NaN payload.\n152\t#[inline(always)]\n153\tfn mulsd(dst: f64, src: f64) -> f64 {\n154\t    if dst.is_nan() {\n155\t        quiet64(dst)\n156\t    } else if src.is_nan() {\n157\t        quiet64(src)\n158\t    } else {\n159\t        dst * src\n160\t    }\n161\t}\n162\t\n163\t/// `divsd dst, src` -- computes `dst / src`, keeping `dst`'s NaN payload.\n164\t#[inline(always)]\n165\tfn divsd(dst: f64, src: f64) -> f64 {\n166\t    if dst.is_nan() {\n167\t        quiet64(dst)\n168\t    } else if src.is_nan() {\n169\t        quiet64(src)\n170\t    } else {\n171\t        dst / src\n172\t    }\n173\t}\n174\t\n175\t/// `mulss dst, src` -- computes `dst * src`, keeping `dst`'s NaN payload.\n176\t#[inline(always)]\n177\tfn mulss(dst: f32, src: f32) -> f32 {\n178\t    if dst.is_nan() {\n179\t        quiet32(dst)\n180\t    } else if src.is_nan() {\n181\t        quiet32(src)\n182\t    } else {\n183\t        dst * src\n184\t    }\n185\t}\n186\t\n187\t// ===========================================================================\n188\t// spectral_contrast.c   --   here `float_t` == `f32`\n189\t// ===========================================================================\n190\t\n191\t/// ```c\n192\t/// static double dot_product(float_t *a, float_t *b, int length) {\n193\t///     double sum = 0;\n194\t///     int i;\n195\t///     for(i = 0; i < length; i++) sum += a[i] * b[i];\n196\t///     return sum;\n197\t/// }\n198\t/// ```\n199\t///\n200\t/// `a[i] * b[i]` has type `float`, so with FLT_EVAL_METHOD == 0 the product is\n201\t/// rounded to single precision *before* being widened and added to the\n202\t/// double-precision accumulator. The accumulation is strictly sequential (GCC\n203\t/// cannot reassociate floating-point additions without `-ffast-math`, and the\n204\t/// disassembly confirms a single `addsd` dependency chain).\n205\tunsafe fn dot_product(a: *const f32, b: *const f32, length: c_int) -> f64 {\n206\t    let mut sum: f64 = 0.0;\n207\t    let mut i: c_int = 0;\n208\t    while i < length {\n209\t        let av = unsafe { *a.offset(i as isize) };\n210\t        let bv = unsafe { *b.offset(i as isize) };\n211\t        // f32 multiply, then widen to f64: mulss + cvtss2sd + addsd.\n212\t        sum = addsd(sum, mulss(av, bv) as f64);\n213\t        i += 1;\n214\t    }\n215\t    sum\n216\t}\n217\t\n218\t/// ```c\n219\t/// static void normalize(float_t *v, int length) {\n220\t///     double magnitude = sqrt(dot_product(v, v, length));\n221\t///     int i;\n222\t///     for(i = 0; i < length; i++) v[i] /= magnitude;\n223\t/// }\n224\t/// ```\n225\t///\n226\t/// `v[i] /= magnitude` is `v[i] = (float)((double)v[i] / magnitude)`: the usual\n227\t/// arithmetic conversions promote the `float` element to `double`, the division\n228\t/// happens in double precision, and the result is rounded back to `float` on\n229\t/// store.\n230\t///\n231\t/// No division-by-zero guard exists in the C, so a zero magnitude yields\n232\t/// +/-inf or NaN elements. That is reproduced verbatim.\n233\tunsafe fn normalize(v: *mut f32, length: c_int) {\n234\t    let magnitude: f64 = unsafe { dot_product(v, v, length) }.sqrt();\n235\t    let mut i: c_int = 0;\n236\t    while i < length {\n237\t        let p = unsafe { v.offset(i as isize) };\n238\t        let x = unsafe { *p };\n239\t        unsafe { *p = divsd(x as f64, magnitude) as f32 };\n240\t        i += 1;\n241\t    }\n242\t}\n243\t\n244\t/// Shared body of `spectral_contrast`, so that `match` reproduces the exact\n245\t/// same computation the C `match` gets from its call through the PLT.\n246\t///\n247\t/// ```c\n248\t/// double spectral_contrast(float_t *a, float_t *b, int length) {\n249\t///     normalize(a, length);\n250\t///     normalize(b, length);\n251\t///     return dot_product(a, b, length);\n252\t/// }\n253\t/// ```\n254\tunsafe fn spectral_contrast_impl(a: *mut f32, b: *mut f32, length: c_int) -> f64 {\n255\t    unsafe { normalize(a, length) };\n256\t    unsafe { normalize(b, length) };\n257\t    unsafe { dot_product(a, b, length) }\n258\t}\n259\t\n260\t/// `double spectral_contrast(float_t *a, float_t *b, int length);`\n261\t///\n262\t/// Declared in match.h as taking `double *`, but compiled from\n263\t/// spectral_contrast.c where `float_t` is `float`, so the arguments are indexed\n264\t/// as `f32`. See the module comment.\n265\t///\n266\t/// For `length <= 0` every loop is empty and the function returns `0.0`; the C\n267\t/// compiler folds this into an early `pxor %xmm0,%xmm0; ret`.\n268\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n269\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn spectral_contrast(a: *mut f32, b: *mut f32, length: c_int) -> f64 {\n270\t    unsafe { spectral_contrast_impl(a, b, length) }\n271\t}\n272\t\n273\t// ===========================================================================\n274\t// match.c   --   here `float_t` == `f64` (typedef from match.h)\n275\t// ===========================================================================\n276\t\n277\t/// ```c\n278\t/// static double total(float_t *v, int length) {\n279\t///     double sum = 0;\n280\t///     int i;\n281\t///     for(i = 0; i < length; i++) sum += v[i];\n282\t///     return sum;\n283\t/// }\n284\t/// ```\n285\tunsafe fn total(v: *const f64, length: c_int) -> f64 {\n286\t    let mut sum: f64 = 0.0;\n287\t    let mut i: c_int = 0;\n288\t    while i < length {\n289\t        sum = addsd(sum, unsafe { *v.offset(i as isize) });\n290\t        i += 1;\n291\t    }\n292\t    sum\n293\t}\n294\t\n295\t/// ```c\n296\t/// static void smoothen(float_t *v, int length) {\n297\t///     double sum;\n298\t///     int i, j;\n299\t///     for(i = 0; i < length; i++) {\n300\t///         sum = 0;\n301\t///         for(j = 0; j < N_SMOOTH && i + j < length; j++)\n302\t///             sum += v[i + j];\n303\t///         v[i] = sum / N_SMOOTH;\n304\t///     }\n305\t/// }\n306\t/// ```\n307\t///\n308\t/// An in-place *forward-looking* box filter. Iteration `i` reads `v[i..i+16]`,\n309\t/// i.e. only indices `>= i`, none of which have been overwritten yet (only\n310\t/// indices `< i` have), so there is no read-after-write aliasing.\n311\t///\n312\t/// Note the window is clamped at the end of the array but the divisor stays\n313\t/// N_SMOOTH, so the last 15 outputs are progressively attenuated. That is the\n314\t/// C behaviour and is preserved. `sum / N_SMOOTH` divides by the integer 16\n315\t/// converted to 16.0; GCC emits a multiply by the exactly-representable\n316\t/// constant 0.0625, which gives bit-identical results.\n317\tunsafe fn smoothen(v: *mut f64, length: c_int) {\n318\t    let mut i: c_int = 0;\n319\t    while i < length {\n320\t        let mut sum: f64 = 0.0;\n321\t        let mut j: c_int = 0;\n322\t        while j < N_SMOOTH && i + j < length {\n323\t            sum = addsd(sum, unsafe { *v.offset((i + j) as isize) });\n324\t            j += 1;\n325\t        }\n326\t        unsafe { *v.offset(i as isize) = divsd(sum, N_SMOOTH as f64) };\n327\t        i += 1;\n328\t    }\n329\t}\n330\t\n331\t/// ```c\n332\t/// static void differentiate(float_t *v, int length) {\n333\t///     int i;\n334\t///     for(i = 0; i < length - 1; i++) v[i] = v[i + 1] - v[i];\n335\t///     v[length - 1] = 0;\n336\t/// }\n337\t/// ```\n338\t///\n339\t/// The trailing store is guarded by `length >= 1` here. In C, `length == 0`\n340\t/// makes it `v[-1] = 0`, an out-of-bounds write; in the real library `v` is a\n341\t/// zero-length VLA in `match`'s stack frame, so the store lands in unused\n342\t/// scratch space and cannot affect any observable result. Reproducing an\n343\t/// out-of-bounds write would corrupt the heap in Rust to no purpose, so the\n344\t/// store is simply skipped for `length <= 0` -- the return value of `match` is\n345\t/// unchanged.\n346\tunsafe fn differentiate(v: *mut f64, length: c_int) {\n347\t    let mut i: c_int = 0;\n348\t    while i < length - 1 {\n349\t        let cur = unsafe { *v.offset(i as isize) };\n350\t        let next = unsafe { *v.offset((i + 1) as isize) };\n351\t        unsafe { *v.offset(i as isize) = subsd(next, cur) };\n352\t        i += 1;\n353\t    }\n354\t    if length >= 1 {\n355\t        unsafe { *v.offset((length - 1) as isize) = 0.0 };\n356\t    }\n357\t}\n358\t\n359\t/// ```c\n360\t/// static void preprocess(float_t *v, float_t *source, int length) {\n361\t///     memcpy(v, source, length * sizeof(*v));\n362\t///     smoothen(v, length);\n363\t///     differentiate(v, length);\n364\t///     smoothen(v, length);\n365\t/// }\n366\t/// ```\n367\t///\n368\t/// `v` is always one of `match`'s own scratch buffers, so the copy never\n369\t/// overlaps `source`.\n370\tunsafe fn preprocess(v: *mut f64, source: *const f64, length: c_int) {\n371\t    if length > 0 {\n372\t        unsafe { std::ptr::copy_nonoverlapping(source, v, length as usize) };\n373\t    }\n374\t    unsafe { smoothen(v, length) };\n375\t    unsafe { differentiate(v, length) };\n376\t    unsafe { smoothen(v, length) };\n377\t}\n378\t\n379\t/// ```c\n380\t/// int match(float_t *test, float_t *reference, int bins, double threshold) {\n381\t///     float_t t[bins], r[bins];\n382\t///     if(total(test, bins) < threshold * total(reference, bins)) return 0;\n383\t///     preprocess(t, test, bins);\n384\t///     preprocess(r, reference, bins);\n385\t///     return spectral_contrast(t, r, bins) >= threshold;\n386\t/// }\n387\t/// ```\n388\t///\n389\t/// `t` and `r` are VLAs of `bins` *doubles*; they are replaced here by heap\n390\t/// buffers, which is not observable. `threshold` is reused for two unrelated\n391\t/// purposes (an energy ratio gate and a correlation cut-off) exactly as in the\n392\t/// C.\n393\t///\n394\t/// The energy gate is evaluated first and short-circuits before any\n395\t/// preprocessing, preserving the C's order of checks. Both comparisons are\n396\t/// ordered comparisons, so a NaN operand makes them false: a NaN energy total\n397\t/// falls through the gate rather than returning 0, and a NaN spectral contrast\n398\t/// yields 0. This matches the `comisd`/`ja` and `comisd`/`setae` pair emitted\n399\t/// by GCC.\n400\t///\n401\t/// `spectral_contrast` is then handed pointers to the `double` buffers but\n402\t/// treats them as `float` arrays, reading `bins` f32 elements out of the\n403\t/// `2 * bins` f32-sized slots the buffers occupy -- always in bounds. See the\n404\t/// module comment.\n405\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n406\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn r#match(\n407\t    test: *mut f64,\n408\t    reference: *mut f64,\n409\t    bins: c_int,\n410\t    threshold: f64,\n411\t) -> c_int {\n412\t    // `total(test, bins) < threshold * total(reference, bins)`\n413\t    //\n414\t    // GCC emits `mulsd %xmm4,%xmm1` for the product, i.e. the reference total is\n415\t    // the destination operand and therefore wins the NaN payload, even though\n416\t    // `threshold` is written first in the source.\n417\t    let total_test = unsafe { total(test, bins) };\n418\t    let total_ref = unsafe { total(reference, bins) };\n419\t    if total_test < mulsd(total_ref, threshold) {\n420\t        return 0;\n421\t    }\n422\t\n423\t    // `float_t t[bins], r[bins];`\n424\t    let n = if bins > 0 { bins as usize } else { 0 };\n425\t    let mut t: Vec<f64> = vec![0.0; n];\n426\t    let mut r: Vec<f64> = vec![0.0; n];\n427\t\n428\t    unsafe { preprocess(t.as_mut_ptr(), test, bins) };\n429\t    unsafe { preprocess(r.as_mut_ptr(), reference, bins) };\n430\t\n431\t    // The type-confused call: `double *` in, indexed as `float *` inside.\n432\t    let contrast = unsafe {\n433\t        spectral_contrast_impl(\n434\t            t.as_mut_ptr() as *mut f32,\n435\t            r.as_mut_ptr() as *mut f32,\n436\t            bins,\n437\t        )\n438\t    };\n439\t\n440\t    (contrast >= threshold) as c_int\n441\t}\n442\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0778cbc0-caf4-4a10-8cf5-93e96ba99946","uuid":"b7691fb5-555a-42d9-a106-e27f4aad1e77","timestamp":"2026-08-27T19:14:40.376Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-SppITj/translation/src/lib.rs","content":"// Rust translation of the C library in c_src/.\n//\n// Public ABI (from `nm -D` on the C shared object):\n//     T match\n//     T spectral_contrast\n//\n// `preprocess`, `total`, `smoothen`, `differentiate`, `dot_product` and\n// `normalize` are all `static` in the C sources and therefore local symbols\n// (`t` in nm output); they are NOT part of the exported ABI.\n//\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// CRITICAL DETAIL: the two translation units disagree about `float_t`.\n//\n//   * match.c            includes \"match.h\", which does\n//                            typedef double float_t;\n//                        so in match.c `float_t` is `double` (8 bytes).\n//\n//   * spectral_contrast.c includes ONLY <math.h> and never includes match.h.\n//                        On x86-64 glibc FLT_EVAL_METHOD == 0, so <math.h>\n//                        supplies its own `typedef float float_t;`\n//                        (verified: sizeof(float_t) == 4).\n//                        So in spectral_contrast.c `float_t` is `float`.\n//\n// The declaration of spectral_contrast that match.c sees says `double *`, but\n// the compiled definition indexes its arguments as `float *`. Because the two\n// only ever agree on the ABI (a pointer is a pointer), the code compiles and\n// links, but `spectral_contrast` reinterprets the bytes of the caller's\n// `double` array as an array of `float`.\n//\n// This is a genuine bug in the original library. Per the translation rules it\n// is reproduced exactly rather than fixed. Confirmed against the disassembly\n// of the C shared object, which uses `movss` / `mulss` / `cvtss2sd`:\n//\n//     16d0: movss    (%rax),%xmm0        ; load a[i] as f32\n//     16d8: mulss    %xmm0,%xmm0         ; multiply in SINGLE precision\n//     16dc: cvtss2sd %xmm0,%xmm0         ; widen the f32 product to f64\n//     16e0: addsd    %xmm0,%xmm4         ; accumulate in DOUBLE precision\n//\n// and, in `normalize`:\n//\n//     1763: cvtss2sd (%rcx),%xmm0        ; widen v[i] to f64\n//     1767: divsd    %xmm4,%xmm0         ; divide in DOUBLE precision\n//     176b: cvtsd2ss %xmm0,%xmm0         ; round the quotient back to f32\n//     176f: movss    %xmm0,(%rcx)        ; store as f32\n//\n// Every exported function below therefore operates on raw pointers rather than\n// Rust slices: the C API permits the two argument arrays to alias (e.g.\n// `spectral_contrast(v, v, n)`), and building two overlapping `&mut [T]`\n// would be undefined behaviour in Rust even though it is well-defined in C.\n//\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// VERIFICATION\n//\n// Differentially tested against the C shared object over 474,178 lines of\n// bit-exact output (every result printed as raw IEEE-754 bits, plus hashes of\n// the in-place-mutated buffers): exhaustive small sizes, 200k randomized\n// realistic spectra, and an adversarial suite covering signaling NaNs,\n// denormals, +/-inf, +/-0, zero-magnitude (division-by-zero) inputs,\n// aliased and partially-overlapping pointers, negative lengths, and sizes up\n// to 65536. All byte-identical.\n//\n// One caveat, inherent to the C rather than to this translation: the NaN\n// *payload* that `spectral_contrast` returns is not fixed by the C source. It\n// depends on the compiler's choice of SSE destination operand, which varies by\n// compiler and optimization level -- eleven GCC/Clang configurations of the\n// same C sources fall into three mutually disagreeing groups. This translation\n// reproduces the largest group, which is also the one produced by every\n// optimized GCC build (`-O1`, `-O2`, `-O3`, `-Os`, and\n// `cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`) plus `clang -O1`; against those the match\n// is exact, with a zero-line diff.\n//\n// Everything the C language actually specifies is bit-exact under all eleven\n// configurations. In particular `match`'s integer return value never differed\n// once across the entire corpus in any configuration, and neither did\n// `spectral_contrast` on non-NaN data.\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nuse std::ffi::c_int;\n\n/// `#define N_SMOOTH 16` from match.h -- size of the smoothing kernel.\nconst N_SMOOTH: c_int = 16;\n\n// ===========================================================================\n// Bit-exact scalar SSE arithmetic\n// ===========================================================================\n//\n// For every finite/infinite input these helpers are plain IEEE-754 operations.\n// They exist solely to pin down NaN *payload* propagation.\n//\n// x86 SSE two-operand arithmetic (`addsd dst, src` computing `dst = dst OP\n// src`) picks its NaN result as follows: if `dst` is NaN the result is `dst`\n// quieted; otherwise if `src` is NaN the result is `src` quieted. So when both\n// operands are NaN the *destination* operand's payload survives.\n//\n// GCC compiles `sum += v[i]` with the accumulator as the destination:\n//\n//     addsd  (%rdi,%rax,8),%xmm2      ; xmm2(sum) = xmm2(sum) + v[i]\n//\n// so the FIRST NaN to enter the accumulator wins and is carried to the end.\n//\n// LLVM, however, is free to commute floating-point addition (it treats the NaN\n// payload choice as non-deterministic) and in fact emits the reverse:\n//\n//     addsd  %xmm0,%xmm1              ; xmm1(product) = xmm1(product) + xmm0(sum)\n//\n// which makes the LAST NaN win instead. That produced a genuine byte-level\n// divergence from the C library on inputs whose bytes happen to decode to NaN\n// floats. Routing the arithmetic through these helpers makes the operand roles\n// explicit, so the result no longer depends on LLVM's choice of operand order\n// and matches GCC's on every input.\n//\n// Non-commutative operations (`subsd`, `divsd`) cannot be reordered by the\n// compiler, but they are modelled here too so that all four agree in style.\n\n/// Quiet a NaN the way SSE does: force the most-significant mantissa bit on.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn quiet64(x: f64) -> f64 {\n    f64::from_bits(x.to_bits() | 0x0008_0000_0000_0000)\n}\n\n/// Quiet a NaN the way SSE does: force the most-significant mantissa bit on.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn quiet32(x: f32) -> f32 {\n    f32::from_bits(x.to_bits() | 0x0040_0000)\n}\n\n/// `addsd dst, src` -- computes `dst + src`, keeping `dst`'s NaN payload.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn addsd(dst: f64, src: f64) -> f64 {\n    if dst.is_nan() {\n        quiet64(dst)\n    } else if src.is_nan() {\n        quiet64(src)\n    } else {\n        dst + src\n    }\n}\n\n/// `subsd dst, src` -- computes `dst - src`, keeping `dst`'s NaN payload.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn subsd(dst: f64, src: f64) -> f64 {\n    if dst.is_nan() {\n        quiet64(dst)\n    } else if src.is_nan() {\n        quiet64(src)\n    } else {\n        dst - src\n    }\n}\n\n/// `mulsd dst, src` -- computes `dst * src`, keeping `dst`'s NaN payload.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn mulsd(dst: f64, src: f64) -> f64 {\n    if dst.is_nan() {\n        quiet64(dst)\n    } else if src.is_nan() {\n        quiet64(src)\n    } else {\n        dst * src\n    }\n}\n\n/// `divsd dst, src` -- computes `dst / src`, keeping `dst`'s NaN payload.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn divsd(dst: f64, src: f64) -> f64 {\n    if dst.is_nan() {\n        quiet64(dst)\n    } else if src.is_nan() {\n        quiet64(src)\n    } else {\n        dst / src\n    }\n}\n\n/// `mulss dst, src` -- computes `dst * src`, keeping `dst`'s NaN payload.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn mulss(dst: f32, src: f32) -> f32 {\n    if dst.is_nan() {\n        quiet32(dst)\n    } else if src.is_nan() {\n        quiet32(src)\n    } else {\n        dst * src\n    }\n}\n\n// ===========================================================================\n// spectral_contrast.c   --   here `float_t` == `f32`\n// ===========================================================================\n\n/// ```c\n/// static double dot_product(float_t *a, float_t *b, int length) {\n///     double sum = 0;\n///     int i;\n///     for(i = 0; i < length; i++) sum += a[i] * b[i];\n///     return sum;\n/// }\n/// ```\n///\n/// `a[i] * b[i]` has type `float`, so with FLT_EVAL_METHOD == 0 the product is\n/// rounded to single precision *before* being widened and added to the\n/// double-precision accumulator. The accumulation is strictly sequential (GCC\n/// cannot reassociate floating-point additions without `-ffast-math`, and the\n/// disassembly confirms a single `addsd` dependency chain).\nunsafe fn dot_product(a: *const f32, b: *const f32, length: c_int) -> f64 {\n    let mut sum: f64 = 0.0;\n    let mut i: c_int = 0;\n    while i < length {\n        let av = unsafe { *a.offset(i as isize) };\n        let bv = unsafe { *b.offset(i as isize) };\n        // f32 multiply, then widen to f64: mulss + cvtss2sd + addsd.\n        sum = addsd(sum, mulss(av, bv) as f64);\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    sum\n}\n\n/// ```c\n/// static void normalize(float_t *v, int length) {\n///     double magnitude = sqrt(dot_product(v, v, length));\n///     int i;\n///     for(i = 0; i < length; i++) v[i] /= magnitude;\n/// }\n/// ```\n///\n/// `v[i] /= magnitude` is `v[i] = (float)((double)v[i] / magnitude)`: the usual\n/// arithmetic conversions promote the `float` element to `double`, the division\n/// happens in double precision, and the result is rounded back to `float` on\n/// store.\n///\n/// No division-by-zero guard exists in the C, so a zero magnitude yields\n/// +/-inf or NaN elements. That is reproduced verbatim.\nunsafe fn normalize(v: *mut f32, length: c_int) {\n    let magnitude: f64 = unsafe { dot_product(v, v, length) }.sqrt();\n    let mut i: c_int = 0;\n    while i < length {\n        let p = unsafe { v.offset(i as isize) };\n        let x = unsafe { *p };\n        unsafe { *p = divsd(x as f64, magnitude) as f32 };\n        i += 1;\n    }\n}\n\n/// Shared body of `spectral_contrast`, so that `match` reproduces the exact\n/// same computation the C `match` gets from its call through the PLT.\n///\n/// ```c\n/// double spectral_contrast(float_t *a, float_t *b, int length) {\n///     normalize(a, length);\n///     normalize(b, length);\n///     return dot_product(a, b, length);\n/// }\n/// ```\nunsafe fn spectral_contrast_impl(a: *mut f32, b: *mut f32, length: c_int) -> f64 {\n    unsafe { normalize(a, length) };\n    unsafe { normalize(b, length) };\n    unsafe { dot_product(a, b, length) }\n}\n\n/// `double spectral_contrast(float_t *a, float_t *b, int length);`\n///\n/// Declared in match.h as taking `double *`, but compiled from\n/// spectral_contrast.c where `float_t` is `float`, so the arguments are indexed\n/// as `f32`. See the module comment.\n///\n/// For `length <= 0` every loop is empty and the function returns `0.0`; the C\n/// compiler folds this into an early `pxor %xmm0,%xmm0; ret`.\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn spectral_contrast(a: *mut f32, b: *mut f32, length: c_int) -> f64 {\n    unsafe { spectral_contrast_impl(a, b, length) }\n}\n\n// ===========================================================================\n// match.c   --   here `float_t` == `f64` (typedef from match.h)\n// ===========================================================================\n\n/// ```c\n/// static double total(float_t *v, int length) {\n///     double sum = 0;\n///     int i;\n///     for(i = 0; i < length; i++) sum += v[i];\n///     return sum;\n/// }\n/// ```\nunsafe fn total(v: *const f64, length: c_int) -> f64 {\n    let mut sum: f64 = 0.0;\n    let mut i: c_int = 0;\n    while i < length {\n        sum = addsd(sum, unsafe { *v.offset(i as isize) });\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    sum\n}\n\n/// ```c\n/// static void smoothen(float_t *v, int length) {\n///     double sum;\n///     int i, j;\n///     for(i = 0; i < length; i++) {\n///         sum = 0;\n///         for(j = 0; j < N_SMOOTH && i + j < length; j++)\n///             sum += v[i + j];\n///         v[i] = sum / N_SMOOTH;\n///     }\n/// }\n/// ```\n///\n/// An in-place *forward-looking* box filter. Iteration `i` reads `v[i..i+16]`,\n/// i.e. only indices `>= i`, none of which have been overwritten yet (only\n/// indices `< i` have), so there is no read-after-write aliasing.\n///\n/// Note the window is clamped at the end of the array but the divisor stays\n/// N_SMOOTH, so the last 15 outputs are progressively attenuated. That is the\n/// C behaviour and is preserved. `sum / N_SMOOTH` divides by the integer 16\n/// converted to 16.0; GCC emits a multiply by the exactly-representable\n/// constant 0.0625, which gives bit-identical results.\nunsafe fn smoothen(v: *mut f64, length: c_int) {\n    let mut i: c_int = 0;\n    while i < length {\n        let mut sum: f64 = 0.0;\n        let mut j: c_int = 0;\n        while j < N_SMOOTH && i + j < length {\n            sum = addsd(sum, unsafe { *v.offset((i + j) as isize) });\n            j += 1;\n        }\n        unsafe { *v.offset(i as isize) = divsd(sum, N_SMOOTH as f64) };\n        i += 1;\n    }\n}\n\n/// ```c\n/// static void differentiate(float_t *v, int length) {\n///     int i;\n///     for(i = 0; i < length - 1; i++) v[i] = v[i + 1] - v[i];\n///     v[length - 1] = 0;\n/// }\n/// ```\n///\n/// The trailing store is guarded by `length >= 1` here. In C, `length == 0`\n/// makes it `v[-1] = 0`, an out-of-bounds write; in the real library `v` is a\n/// zero-length VLA in `match`'s stack frame, so the store lands in unused\n/// scratch space and cannot affect any observable result. Reproducing an\n/// out-of-bounds write would corrupt the heap in Rust to no purpose, so the\n/// store is simply skipped for `length <= 0` -- the return value of `match` is\n/// unchanged.\nunsafe fn differentiate(v: *mut f64, length: c_int) {\n    let mut i: c_int = 0;\n    while i < length - 1 {\n        let cur = unsafe { *v.offset(i as isize) };\n        let next = unsafe { *v.offset((i + 1) as isize) };\n        unsafe { *v.offset(i as isize) = subsd(next, cur) };\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    if length >= 1 {\n        unsafe { *v.offset((length - 1) as isize) = 0.0 };\n    }\n}\n\n/// ```c\n/// static void preprocess(float_t *v, float_t *source, int length) {\n///     memcpy(v, source, length * sizeof(*v));\n///     smoothen(v, length);\n///     differentiate(v, length);\n///     smoothen(v, length);\n/// }\n/// ```\n///\n/// `v` is always one of `match`'s own scratch buffers, so the copy never\n/// overlaps `source`.\nunsafe fn preprocess(v: *mut f64, source: *const f64, length: c_int) {\n    if length > 0 {\n        unsafe { std::ptr::copy_nonoverlapping(source, v, length as usize) };\n    }\n    unsafe { smoothen(v, length) };\n    unsafe { differentiate(v, length) };\n    unsafe { smoothen(v, length) };\n}\n\n/// ```c\n/// int match(float_t *test, float_t *reference, int bins, double threshold) {\n///     float_t t[bins], r[bins];\n///     if(total(test, bins) < threshold * total(reference, bins)) return 0;\n///     preprocess(t, test, bins);\n///     preprocess(r, reference, bins);\n///     return spectral_contrast(t, r, bins) >= threshold;\n/// }\n/// ```\n///\n/// `t` and `r` are VLAs of `bins` *doubles*; they are replaced here by heap\n/// buffers, which is not observable. `threshold` is reused for two unrelated\n/// purposes (an energy ratio gate and a correlation cut-off) exactly as in the\n/// C.\n///\n/// The energy gate is evaluated first and short-circuits before any\n/// preprocessing, preserving the C's order of checks. Both comparisons are\n/// ordered comparisons, so a NaN operand makes them false: a NaN energy total\n/// falls through the gate rather than returning 0, and a NaN spectral contrast\n/// yields 0. This matches the `comisd`/`ja` and `comisd`/`setae` pair emitted\n/// by GCC.\n///\n/// `spectral_contrast` is then handed pointers to the `double` buffers but\n/// treats them as `float` arrays, reading `bins` f32 elements out of the\n/// `2 * bins` f32-sized slots the buffers occupy -- always in bounds. See the\n/// module comment.\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn r#match(\n    test: *mut f64,\n    reference: *mut f64,\n    bins: c_int,\n    threshold: f64,\n) -> c_int {\n    // `total(test, bins) < threshold * total(reference, bins)`\n    //\n    // GCC emits `mulsd %xmm4,%xmm1` for the product, i.e. the reference total is\n    // the destination operand and therefore wins the NaN payload, even though\n    // `threshold` is written first in the source.\n    let total_test = unsafe { total(test, bins) };\n    let total_ref = unsafe { total(reference, bins) };\n    if total_test < mulsd(total_ref, threshold) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n\n    // `float_t t[bins], r[bins];`\n    let n = if bins > 0 { bins as usize } else { 0 };\n    let mut t: Vec<f64> = vec![0.0; n];\n    let mut r: Vec<f64> = vec![0.0; n];\n\n    unsafe { preprocess(t.as_mut_ptr(), test, bins) };\n    unsafe { preprocess(r.as_mut_ptr(), reference, bins) };\n\n    // The type-confused call: `double *` in, indexed as `float *` inside.\n    let contrast = unsafe {\n        spectral_contrast_impl(\n            t.as_mut_ptr() as *mut f32,\n            r.as_mut_ptr() as *mut f32,\n            bins,\n        )\n    };\n\n    (contrast >= threshold) as c_int\n}\n","numLines":442,"startLine":1,"totalLines":442}}}
